      │\n │       creating a polished and professional-feeling tool.                                    │\n │    71                                                                                       │\n │    72 In summary, this is a high-quality, ambitious project with a solid architectural      │\n │       foundation. Its main weaknesses are not in its vision or features, but in the         │\n │       \"scaffolding\" that ensures long-term quality and maintainability—namely, automated    │\n │       testing and static typing. Addressing these areas would elevate it from a impressive  │\n │       prototype to a truly robust and professional-grade application.                       │\n ╰─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯\n✦ The report has been saved to /gemini-docs/CODEBASE_REVIEW.md. I am ready for your next instruction.            \n                                                                                                                 \n╭────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮       \n│  > write a GEMINI.md that is designed to support this project. Your first job will be to implement an │        \n│     automated testing strategy. I'm familiar with Playwrite so we can employ that if needed           │        \n╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯       \n                                                                                                                 \n✦ Excellent. I will create a GEMINI.md file outlining my role and the plan to implement an automated             \n  testing strategy using Playwright. This document will serve as our roadmap.                                    \n ╭─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮                 \n │ ✔ WriteFile Writing to GEMINI.md                                                           │                  \n │                                                                                             │                 \n │      1 # ♊ **Gemini Support Plan: three-loader**                                           │                 \n │      2                                                                                      │                 \n │      3 This document outlines the development and support plan provided by Gemini for the   │                 \n │        `three-loader` project. It is based on the initial [Codebase Review](                │                 \n │        ./gemini-docs/CODEBASE_REVIEW.md) and will serve as a living document to track       │                 \n │        tasks and priorities.                                                                │                 \n │      4                                                                                      │                 \n │      5 ## **Immediate Priority: Automated Testing Strategy**                                │                 \n │      6                                                                                      │                 \n │      7 Based on the codebase review, the highest priority is to establish an automated      │\n │        testing framework. This will provide a safety net for future development, prevent    │\n │        regressions, and enable safe refactoring.                                            │\n │      8                                                                                      │\n │      9 We will use **Playwright** for end-to-end (E2E) testing, as it excels at simulating  │\n │        real user interactions in a browser environment, which is perfect for this highly    │\n │        interactive application.                                                             │\n │     10                                                                                      │\n │     11 ---                                                                                  │\n │     12                                                                                      │\n │     13 ### **Phase 1: Playwright Setup & Initial Smoke Test**                               │\n │     14                                                                                      │\n │     15 **Goal:** Install, configure, and validate the testing framework.                    │\n │     16                                                                                      │\n │     17 1.  **Install Playwright:**                                                          │\n │     18     *   Add the necessary development dependency to `package.json`.                  │\n │     19     *   Command: `npm install --save-dev @playwright/test`                           │\n │     20                                                                                      │\n │     21 2.  **Configure Playwright:**                                                        │\n │     22     *   Create `playwright.config.js` at the project root.                           │\n │     23     *   Configure the `webServer` option to automatically launch the Vite dev        │\n │        server (`npm run dev`) before running tests. This ensures tests run against a live,  │\n │        build-free version of the app.                                                       │\n │     24     *   Set the base URL to the Vite server address (e.g., `http://localhost:5173`   │\n │        ).                                                                                   │\n │     25                                                                                      │\n │     26 3.  **Create First Test (Smoke Test):**                                              │\n │     27     *   Create a new test file, e.g., `tests/app.spec.js`.                           │\n │     28     *   Write a simple test that:                                                    │\n │     29         *   Navigates to the root URL.                                               │\n │     30         *   Asserts that the page title is correct.                                  │\n │     31         *   Asserts that the main UI panels (e.g., \"Scene Objects\", \"Material        │\n │        Editor\") are visible.                                                                │\n │     32     *   This test will confirm that the application loads without crashing.          │\n │     33                                                                                      │\n │     34 4.  **Update `package.json`:**                                                       │\n │     35     *   Add a new script for running tests: `\"test\": \"playwright test\"`.             │\n │     36                                                                                      │\n │     37 ---                                                                                  │\n │     38                                                                                      │\n │     39 ### **Phase 2: Core Functionality Tests**                                            │\n │     40                                                                                      │\n │     41 **Goal:** Test the fundamental features of loading and creating objects.             │\n │     42                                                                                      │\n │     43 1.  **Test File Loading:**                                                           │\n │     44     *   Create a test that simulates a user dragging and dropping an `.obj` file (   │\n │        `examples/spiked.obj`) onto the viewport.                                            │\n │     45     *   **Assertions:**                                                              │\n │     46         *   Verify that a new item appears in the \"Scene Objects\" list.              │\n │     47         *   Verify that the object count in the scene statistics increases.          │\n │     48         *   (Advanced) Verify that the 3D canvas contains new geometry.              │\n │     49                                                                                      │\n │     50 2.  **Test Primitive Creation:**                                                     │\n │     51     *   Create a test that selects \"Box\" from the primitive dropdown and clicks the  │\n │        \"Add Primitive\" button.                                                              │\n │     52     *   **Assertions:**                                                              │\n │     53         *   Verify that a \"Box\" object appears in the \"Scene Objects\" list.          │\n │     54         *   Verify that the \"Add Primitive\" button becomes disabled after creation.  │\n │     55                                                                                      │\n │     56 ---                                                                                  │\n │     57                                                                                      │\n │     58 ### **Phase 3: UI Interaction & State Change Tests**                                 │\n │     59                                                                                      │\n │     60 **Goal:** Ensure that UI controls correctly modify the state of the 3D objects.      │\n │     61                                                                                      │\n │     62 1.  **Test Material Editor:**                                                        │\n │     63     *   Load an object.                                                              │\n │     64     *   Use Playwright locators to interact with the \"Material Editor\" panel.        │\n │     65     *   Change the color using the color picker.                                     │\n │     66     *   Drag the `roughness` slider.                                                 │\n │     67     *   **Assertions:** This is tricky without direct access to the Three.js scene.  │\n │        We will use the **bidirectional sync** feature as our validation mechanism. After    │\n │        changing the UI, we will:                                                            │\n │     68         *   Click the \"From UI\" sync button.                                         │\n │     69         *   Assert that the code in the Monaco editor now contains the new color     │\n │        hex value and roughness value.                                                       │\n │     70                                                                                      │\n │     71 2.  **Test Transform Controls:**                                                     │\n │     72     *   Load an object.                                                              │\n │     73     *   Interact with the \"Transform\" panel inputs to change the `position-x`        │\n │        value.                                                                               │\n │     74     *   **Assertions:**                                                              │\n │     75         *   Click the \"From UI\" sync button.                                         │\n │     76         *   Assert that the code in the Monaco editor reflects the new X position.   │\n │     77                                                                                      │\n │     78 ---                                                                                  │\n │     79                                                                                      │\n │     80 ### **Phase 4: Bidirectional Sync Workflow Tests**                                   │\n │     81                                                                                      │\n │     82 **Goal:** Test the most critical and complex feature of the application—the sync     │\n │        between the code and the UI.                                                         │\n │     83                                                                                      │\n │     84 1.  **Test UI-to-Code Sync:**                                                        │\n │     85     *   This is covered by the tests in Phase 3, which use the code editor's state   │\n │        as the ground truth for UI changes.                                                  │\n │     86                                                                                      │\n │     87 2.  **Test Code-to-UI Sync:**                                                        │\n │     88     *   Load an object.                                                              │\n │     89     *   Programmatically clear the Monaco editor and fill it with a simple piece of  │\n │        code that creates a `MeshStandardMaterial` with a unique color (e.g., `0xff00ff`).   │\n │     90     *   Click the \"To UI\" sync button.                                               │\n │     91     *   **Assertions:**                                                              │\n │     92         *   Assert that the color picker in the \"Material Editor\" now shows the new  │\n │        color (`#ff00ff`).                                                                   │\n │     93         *   (Advanced) Take a screenshot of the viewport to visually verify the      │\n │        object's color has changed. Playwright's visual regression testing can be used       │\n │        here.                                                                                │\n │     94                                                                                      │\n │     95 ---                                                                                  │\n │     96                                                                    